meta {
  name: Send contact message
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/contact
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "Ana Silva",
    "email": "ana@example.com",
    "orderNumber": "LPA-00248",
    "message": "Where is my order?"
  }
}

docs {
  # Send contact message

  Accepts a message for the Lpaecomms team. Backs the contact form of the
  mobile About & Contact view; desktop can use it too. Validation is the web
  contact form's own (`lib/contactValidation.js`), so every client accepts
  and rejects the same input.

  **Nothing is delivered yet.** Like the web form, an accepted message is
  validated and acknowledged but not emailed or stored — delivery is a
  separate integration. That is why success is `202 Accepted`.

  ## Auth

  None. This endpoint is public.

  ## Body (JSON object)

  - `name` (string, required) — at most 100 characters.
  - `email` (string, required) — at most 254 characters, `name@domain.tld`.
  - `orderNumber` (string, optional) — at most 20 characters, `LPA-` plus digits.
  - `message` (string, required) — at most 2000 characters.

  Values are trimmed before validation. Unknown fields are ignored.

  ## Responses

  ### 202 Accepted

  { "received": true }

  ### 422 Unprocessable Entity — a field failed validation

  One entry per failing field, in field order, each naming its field.

  {
    "statusCode": 422,
    "error": "Unprocessable Entity",
    "message": "Some fields need attention.",
    "validation": [
      { "field": "email", "message": "Please enter a valid email address." }
    ],
    "url": "/api/v1/contact",
    "method": "POST"
  }

  ### 400 Bad Request — no usable body

  The body is missing, is not JSON, is malformed JSON ("The request body is
  not valid JSON."), or is JSON but not an object ("Send the message as a
  JSON object.").

  {
    "statusCode": 400,
    "error": "Bad Request",
    "message": "Send the message as a JSON object.",
    "validation": [],
    "url": "/api/v1/contact",
    "method": "POST"
  }

  ## CORS

  Cross-origin clients send a preflight `OPTIONS` first; the API answers it
  with `204` and the CORS headers.
}
